<section class="section" id="protection">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="privacy-grid">
      <div class="privacy-copy">
        <div class="eyebrow">Your data. Your boundaries.</div>
        <h2>
          Value shouldn’t come
          <br />
          at the cost of control.
        </h2>
        <p>
          Good data partnerships start with clear permissions. Build the scope,
          handling requirements, and buyer obligations into the agreement—not
          into an afterthought.
        </p>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li>Choose eligible systems, date ranges, and exclusions.</li>
          <li>Define de-identification and review requirements.</li>
          <li>Specify permitted uses and onward-sharing restrictions.</li>
          <li>Agree on retention, deletion, and payment terms.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="privacy-demo">
        <div class="demo-header">
          <span>CONTEXT IN. IDENTIFIERS OUT.</span>
          <span>↗</span>
        </div>
        <div class="demo-card">
          <small>ILLUSTRATIVE SOURCE RECORD</small>
          <p>
            <mark>Alex Chen</mark> resolved the renewal blocker for{" "}
            <mark>Acme Co.</mark> after the support handoff.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="connector" aria-hidden="true">
          ↓
        </div>
        <div class="demo-card safe">
          <small>ILLUSTRATIVE PREPARED RECORD</small>
          <p>
            <mark>PERSON_01</mark> resolved the renewal blocker for{" "}
            <mark>COMPANY_02</mark> after the support handoff.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Synthetic example only. Replacing identifiers is one step, not a
          complete privacy solution. De-identification reduces risk; it cannot
          guarantee that re-identification is impossible.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="values">
      <article>
        <h3>Ownership stays with you.</h3>
        <p>
          A license grants defined usage rights. It doesn’t have to mean selling
          ownership of your underlying data.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>No blanket permissions.</h3>
        <p>
          Buyer access, exclusivity, and future uses belong in the terms you
          review and approve.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Fit before a forecast.</h3>
        <p>
          Value depends on buyer demand, usable history, quality, and rights—not
          a universal price per record.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
